## 3. Conclusions
In summary, the axial O-coordinated $\mathrm{FeN}_{4}$ active site was precisely constructed by using a self-sacrificing O- and N-rich
IRMOF-3. The obtained $\mathrm{FeN}_{4}–\mathrm{O}$ sites were experimentally proved to be much more active than the $\mathrm{FeN}_{4}$ active sites, showing an excellent FE (CO) of $95\%$ at a low applied potential of $-0.50\mathrm{~V~}$ versus RHE. Such performance outperforms previously reported Fe-N-C-based catalysts and most of the reported single atom catalysts at lower overpotential. Meanwhile, DFT simulations revealed that the axial O coordination could not only weaken the binding energy of CO but also inhibit the competitive hydrogen evolution. This work opens a new way for engineering the coordination of $\mathrm{FeN}_{4}$ sites via heteroatom-rich MOFs precursors.
